In a book titled "The Morrow Book of Quotations in American History"
published in 1984 there is a quote that is attributed to Thomas 
Jefferson.  It's as follows:

   There shall be neither slavery nor involuntary servitude in the said
territory, otherwise than in the punishment of crimes whereof the party
shall have been duly convicted.
                     (Northwest Ordinance, 1787)

This is likely to be where the drafters of the 13th ammendment received 
their inspiration for their phraseology.  This statement does not seem 
to imply that only "involuntary servitude" was to be used as punishment
of convicted felons.

Prison at hard labor does qualify as "involuntary servitude" according
to the American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language, 3rd Ed.
To wit: servitude, noun, 1.b. Lack of personal freedom, as to act as one
chooses. 2. Forced labor imposed as a punishment for crime: _penal 
servitude in labor camps._

Slavery is a different kettle of fish. The AmHerDic defines slavery as 
"The state of one bound in servitude as the property of a slaveholder or
household."  A convicted prisoner could be considered the "property" of
the state and therefore a slave in a very narrow use of the word.

The 13th ammendment's purpose appears to serve as a prohibition of 
classic slavery as practiced by the Romans, Arabs, England, and US of A
(as well as others) at various times throughout history.  This requires
thinking of the slave as nothing but chattel.  Which, like cows, may be
bought, sold, bred, and progeny sold at the will of the slaveholder.

This type of slavery is not a valid social contract within the US of A
today.  It can not exist legally.

