My org is miltary by designation and Federal Civilian bureaucrat by default,
howver, I don't have the little stickers on the phone -- yet.  The cover 
of our office phone directory states: "Official DOD telephones are 
subject to monitoring...at all times. ... Use of official DOD telephones 
constitutes  consent to communications security monitoring ... ."  This 
is primairily aimed at not discussing classified information over a 
non-secured line.

Question: If the CERT banner is interpreted literally, how much manpower,
hardware/software and budget resources are being expended to literally
monitor everybodys each and every single keystroke?  

My org's (the US Army Corps of Engineers, North Pacific Division, Seattle
District Office) IMO (Information Management Office) does not have the 
manpower, the budget, or the hardware/software resources to expend playing
these sort of games.  If there were evidence of a hack-in then yes, some
resources would be expended to try to track the perpetrator down and to 
ascertain damage and make corrective measures.  But that's a big _IF_.

Second Question: if CERT can afford (interpreting literally) to play big
brother all of the time, what money pit are they drawing their cash from
and how can I get a piece of the action? :)

Bottom Line: IMHO if I am an authorized user doing authorized things 
then being monitored is essentially irrelevant. And if I'm not an 
authorized user?  Then I pays me nickle, 'n' tykes me chances at grabbing
the gold ring'o'life as I come 'round agin.

